SOAPBOX: With few operators at hand, some remnants of headaches from the
SOAPBOX: ice storm, and scheduling messes from Valentine's Day and life
SOAPBOX: in general... I fell into a good spot. NR4M graciously offered
SOAPBOX: up the full services of the Goat Farm station for me to make an
SOAPBOX: attempt at a decent single-op effort. Given that the station is
SOAPBOX: laid out and equipment positioned for multi-multi operations, it
SOAPBOX: didn't lend itself to last-minute or temporary SO2R fixes the
SOAPBOX: solution was the Classic overlay. I think that not having
SOAPBOX: spotted QSO's and mults constantly streaming past my eyes helped
SOAPBOX: with keeping my hand off the VFO and focus on running, running,
SOAPBOX: running.
SOAPBOX: Strong start on 40, a wise move to pick up some stations on 20,
SOAPBOX: followed by a poor decision to stay on 20 too late. 80 wasn't
SOAPBOX: bad, and went back to 40 before packing in for the night. Came
SOAPBOX: back a few hours later, hopped on 15, and squeezed it until it
SOAPBOX: felt like there was nothing left. Then, the seemingly logical
SOAPBOX: advancement to 20, 40, 80, and back to 40 until the clock ran
SOAPBOX: out. Never even made it to 10, but with the rate on 15 the way
SOAPBOX: it was, I didn't want to think of moving.
SOAPBOX: NR4M told me coming into this that 40 would be the money band,
SOAPBOX: and he was 100% on that.
SOAPBOX: Enormous thanks to everybody in the contest for all the Q's an
SOAPBOX: unimaginably huge thanks to Steve for hosting, allowing me this
SOAPBOX: opportunity and the thrill to operate (and the trust to not
SOAPBOX: break anything) and all the Goat Farm ... associate farmers?
SOAPBOX: Assistant farmers? The crew of jailbirds... I mean operators...
SOAPBOX: that are in and out, helping with various things, that have made
SOAPBOX: this possible.
SOAPBOX: 73! Fred, K4PZZ
